PMIC- Pwer management IC: The e cores can be turned on or off. It has its own power rail.
“Can be turned on or off” means the E-cores can be power gated: when they’re idle or not needed, the chip cuts their power supply entirely. A powered-off core uses almost no energy, not even leakage. Leakage is the current that transistors waste just by being powered, and in modern processes it’s a big part of total power, so shutting a core off is far better than just letting it sit idle.
This turns off the cache associated with the e core as well. The P core can still work independently.
“Its own power rail” means the E-cores are fed by a dedicated supply line at their own voltage, separate from the P-cores’ rail. This ties directly to the PMIC discussion. The PMIC outputs several rails, and one of them goes only to the E-cores. That gives you two things:
Independent voltage: The E-cores can run at a different voltage from the P-cores. Voltage is the biggest lever on power, since dynamic power scales roughly with V² x frequency, so a core doing light work can run at a low voltage and sip power while the P-cores sit at a different level or are off.
Independent on/off: With a shared rail, you can only gate cores with switches inside the chip. A separate rail lets the whole E-core group be shut down or ramped on its own.

These are fixed railed. They have variable rails as well. Example - DRAM Rail.

