The GPU:
Using the new Xe2 engine. It integrates XMX engines to provide better GPU capabilites. Media engine and display engine are part of the Xe2 architecture.It integrates HDMI 2.1 as well as DP 2.1. Has a low power eDP 1.5 channel. They have three display pipelines. Support H.266 VVC decoding.

Xe2
Intel’s second-generation Xe GPU architecture. In Lunar Lake it’s the integrated GPU, on the compute tile, sharing the package and memory with the CPU cores.
XMX engines
XMX (Xe Matrix eXtensions) are dedicated hardware units inside the GPU for matrix multiplication, the core operation of AI and neural-network workloads. They do many multiply-accumulates per cycle on low-precision numbers (INT8, FP16, BF16), much faster and at lower energy than the regular shader cores. This is Intel’s counterpart to Nvidia’s tensor cores. “Better GPU capabilities” really means faster AI work on the GPU, such as upscaling or running ML models. This is also the same idea as the PIM discussion at the start: matrix math is the workload that drives everyone’s interest in bandwidth and efficiency.
Media engine
A fixed-function block that encodes and decodes video (H.264, H.265, AV1, and so on). It’s separate from the shader cores because dedicated hardware does it with much less power. That’s why video playback barely taxes your battery.
Display engine
The block that takes finished frames from memory and sends them to screens: scanning out pixels, scaling, color handling, and driving the output ports. It’s separate from the rendering part of the GPU.
HDMI 2.1, DP 2.1, eDP 1.5
These are the display connection standards the display engine supports:
HDMI 2.1: the TV and monitor standard, with enough bandwidth for high resolutions and refresh rates (for example 4K at 120 Hz).
DP 2.1 (DisplayPort): the same idea for monitors and docks, with even higher bandwidth for high-resolution, high-refresh displays.
eDP 1.5 (embedded DisplayPort): the version for the laptop’s built-in screen, connected internally rather than through a cable. “Low power” here means it has features that save power, such as lowering the refresh rate or not resending the image when the screen content hasn’t changed. That matters for battery life, which is Lunar Lake’s main goal.
Three display pipelines
A pipeline is an independent path that generates the image stream for one display. Three pipelines means it can drive up to three displays at the same time, each with its own resolution and timing (for example, the laptop panel plus two external monitors).
H.266 VVC decoding
H.266 is VVC (Versatile Video Coding), the successor to H.265 (HEVC). It compresses video much better, with roughly half the bitrate for the same quality, as I recall. “Decoding support” means the media engine can play VVC video in hardware, efficiently, rather than burning CPU power on it. Note it says decoding only, not encoding. VVC is not widely used yet, so this is a forward-looking feature.


